{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"c33bf617-35bd-4ff6-a907-5e7d748d7fbc","hash":"74ea3d8c9cb8f6e6bd474a406434c655dbb6fee0d6aa6f48a1194f8ee0a6d7f0","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YN4yUKehACdtoBn4p4JRPg","name":"Edit","input":{"file_path":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/.claude/worktrees/hedgerow-grass/fun/hedgerow/README.md","old_string":"    motion and its shading worked out in the vertex shader, its shadow\n    moving with it), `signs.js` (the occupiers' direction posts where the","new_string":"    motion and its shading worked out in the vertex shader, its shadow\n    moving with it), `chutes.js` (parachutes still caught in the trees\n    along the forest track since the airborne came down over the woods:\n    in three broadleaved trees 5-12 m off the track, 45 m apart, a canopy\n    snagged by its apex and two points either side on the crown's flank\n    turned to the track, the rest hanging down that side in folds over\n    what it meets, its 14 rigging lines in a sheaf below to where a man\n    cut himself free, the two risers dangling; the drape worked out once\n    at load (~0.13 s): a net of the silk's own lengths, crumpling freely,\n    settling over the crown - the body its branches and leaves fill,\n    fitted to the tree model's own points, smaller for bare and thin\n    crowns - its trunk and the ground; pale silk with its 28 gores' seams,\n    stained, torn, soaked dark, swinging out and fluttering where it hangs\n    clear in the gusts that bend the trees, its lines with it, its\n    shadow moving with it under a flare), `signs.js` (the occupiers' direction posts where the"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LMxqFqFTjimE1onhYSZL7v","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/.claude/worktrees/hedgerow-grass && git add fun/hedgerow/js/chutes.js fun/hedgerow/js/main.js fun/hedgerow/README.md && git commit -q -F - <<'EOF'\nHedgerow graphics: parachutes caught in the trees along the forest track\n\nThe forest half of the route was a flat, murky mass at night, with\nnothing pale or lit to catch the eye between flares. Parachutes left\nhanging in the trees since the airborne drop are period-true. They show\nup in the same Normandy nights in MoH Airborne and CoD Vanguard, and the\npale silk takes every flare, fire and flash of lightning.\n\nchutes.js picks three broadleaved trees 5-12 m off the track, 45 m\napart, whose crowns are low enough to see. Each crown is fitted with the\nbody its own branches and leaves fill (an ellipsoid from the model's\npoints, smaller for bare and thin crowns). A canopy net with the silk's\nown lengths, crumpling freely, is snagged by its apex and two points\neither side on the flank facing the track. It is then settled once at\nload over the crown, the trunk and the ground (~0.13 s), so it hangs\ndown that side in folds. Below it, 14 rigging lines run in a sheaf to\nwhere a man cut free, with the two risers dangling. The silk shows its\n28 gores' seams, stains, tears and a ragged hem, and darkens when\nsoaked. Where it hangs clear it swings out and flutters in the gusts,\nwith its lines; its shadow moves with it. About 1.6k + 3k triangles,\ntwo draws.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEOF\ngit log --oneline -3","description":"Commit the parachutes"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
